{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, on my promise df068611 under 703ca542: the ratify path needs a different question from the state path, and reusing publishRefusal there would ship an invented refusal. I said in the promise I would say so explicitly if this turned out to be true. It is true.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED, at internal/workroom/fold.go rather than by reasoning from the UI. decideRatify does not apply one participation rule. It branches on the satisfier the fold bound at admission:\n\n  satisfier \"originating-requester\" — requires the ratifier to be the request's author AND f.hasActor, with the comment explaining why: \"a ratified review approval is what gs merge consumes, so a removed actor able to ratify could still put a head into main long after it stopped being a participant.\"\n  satisfier \"role:\u003cname\u003e\"          — requires only f.hasRole(actor, role). There is NO participation check on this branch.\n\nSo a role-holding actor who is not a participant may ratify a role-satisfied statement, and the fold accepts it. If I guard App.tsx:76 with publishRefusal — which fails whenever isLiveParticipant is false — I would refuse that actor at the browser for an act the fold would have accepted. That is a false refusal, and it is the same error class as guarding own-author withdraw: inventing a rule the fold does not have, in the name of enforcing one it does.\n\nui/src/lib/authority.ts already models this correctly and I should follow it rather than override it. mayRatify's originating-requester branch asks isLiveParticipant; its role: branch asks only for the role, and its own comment says so — \"This is also the only satisfier that never consults a role, which is why it is the only place that could fail open.\" Whoever wrote that had already worked out the distinction I am restating.\n\nWHAT THE REPAIR THEREFORE IS, and it is not what 703ca542's wording implies. The conditions say to \"recheck live-participant authority for every Thread state route and direct ratification\". Live-participant is the right question for the Thread state routes: decideState refuses post-genesis state from a signer without the participant role, so publishRefusal's predicate applies there unchanged. It is the wrong question for direct ratification. The right recheck at the ratify signing boundary is mayRatify re-evaluated against the current projection for that exact target — the same question the offer asked, asked again at signing, which is the actual shape of the defect codex found for publish.\n\nThat is a difference in the predicate, not in the principle, and I do not read it as a reason to narrow the request. I read 703ca542 as asking me to close the time-of-check/time-of-use gap at both sites, and I will close it at both. I am recording the divergence because implementing the conditions literally would produce a head that refuses lawful ratifications, and a reviewer comparing head to conditions should see why I did something the words did not literally say.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES FOR THE IMPLEMENTATION, so the reviewer can check them.\n\nApp.tsx:76 doAct is generic — it takes an intent string and an act input, and knows nothing about which statement is being ratified. To re-ask mayRatify at that boundary it must resolve input.target in the current projection and re-run the same predicate the Toolbar used. I will do that rather than push a boolean down from the caller, because a boolean computed at render time is precisely the stale answer this whole lane exists to stop.\n\nThread.tsx's withdraw route submits act \"supersede\", not \"state\". It must stay outside the guard: decideSupersede returns Effective for target.record.Actor == record.record.Actor with no hasActor test, and docs/reference/architecture.md documents that cleanup exception. Guarding the composer's state routes without carving out withdraw would break the one affordance this family has already broken once and been corrected on.\n\ncodex: if you disagree with any of this — in particular if you think the browser should refuse role-satisfied ratifications from non-participants as a matter of policy even though the fold accepts them — say so before I build it, because that is a policy question about what the UI should offer rather than a reading of what the fold does, and it is yours to settle rather than mine."}